tel 1.0

base legerdemain
import soundness

info   HTML forms derived from data types, and the form-encoded `Query` their submissions decode from
detail
    _Legerdemain_ treats an HTML form as a user interface to a data type. A case class gives a
    form — a labelled widget per field, chosen by the field's type, with nested case classes as
    nested fieldsets — and a submission, a `Query` of key–value pairs, decodes back into the
    case class, with any failure pointing at the field which caused it. The type is the single
    source of truth for both halves, so the form cannot drift from the data it collects.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import formulations.postFormulation
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    case class Person(name: Text, admin: Boolean)
    case class Team(leader: Person, title: Text)

    val form: Html of Flow = elicit[Team](validation = Validation(), submit = t"Save")

    val query = t"leader.name=Ada&leader.admin=on&title=Analysts".as[Query]
    query.as[Team]   // Team(Person(t"Ada", true), t"Analysts")
    ```

    The pieces fit together through three typeclasses. `Elicitable` says which `Widget` a
    field's type is edited with — a `Field` for anything encodable as text, a `Checkbox` for a
    `Boolean`, a `Combobox` for an enumeration, a `Dropdown` for a stored `Reference`.
    `Formulaic` is derived for a product type from its fields' `Elicitable`s and produces the
    form's rows. `Formulation` decides how the form and each row are framed in HTML; the one
    standard choice is `formulations.postFormulation`, imported by name, since `import
    soundness.*` does not import givens.

    The library has two source sets. `legerdemain.query` holds `Query` and `Parametric`: the
    form-encoded key–value structure, with no HTML in it, which the HTTP client and server
    use for URL query strings and form posts. `legerdemain.core` adds the widgets, the
    typeclasses, `elicit`, `edit` and the `formulations` package, and depends on honeycomb's
    HTML. Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella. Telekinesis builds the form
    cycle — render, submit, re-render with errors — on these, as `orchestrate`.

refer legerdemain.Query
refer legerdemain.elicit
refer legerdemain.Formulaic
refer legerdemain.Formulation
refer legerdemain.Elicitable

entry .elicit   renders the form for a type, optionally filled from a submitted `Query`, with its validation messages
    `elicit[Value](query, validation, submit)` renders the whole form for `Value`, which must
    be `Formulaic`, through the `Formulation` given in scope. `query` is what was submitted, to
    show the user's entries again (`Unset` for a blank form); `validation` holds the messages
    to attach to fields, keyed by each field's `Pointer` (`Validation()` for none); and
    `submit` is the submit button's label, or `Unset` for the formulation's default.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import formulations.postFormulation
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    case class Person(name: Text, email: Text)

    val blank = elicit[Person](validation = Validation(), submit = t"Register")

    val submitted = t"name=Ada".as[Query]
    val again = elicit[Person](submitted, Validation(), t"Register")
    ```

    Each field is labelled from its name, split at its capitals and capitalised (`firstName`
    becomes _First name_), and a nested case class becomes a `<fieldset>` with the field's
    label as its legend. A field's widget is named by its dotted path, `leader.name`, which is
    the key it is submitted under.

entry .edit   renders the form for a value, filled with that value's fields
    An extension on any value which is both `Formulaic` and `Encodable in Query`:
    `value.edit(validation, submit)` is the form `elicit` renders, filled with the value's own
    fields encoded as a `Query`, for changing an existing record. It needs a `Formulation` in
    scope.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import formulations.postFormulation

    case class Person(name: Text, email: Text)

    Person(t"Ada", t"ada@example.com").edit(Validation(), t"Update")
    ```

entry .formulations   the standard ways to frame a form in HTML, each a given to import by name
  detail
      How a form and its rows are rendered is a `Formulation` in scope, and `import
      soundness.*` brings none in: one is imported by name, or an application defines its own
      to match its design.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import formulations.postFormulation
      ```
  entry .postFormulation   a plain, unstyled form which posts back to its own URL
      The form is a `<form action="." method="post">` holding the rows and a submit button
      labelled with the `submit` text, or _Submit_ if it is `Unset`. Each row is a `<div>`
      holding a `<p class="alert">` with the field's validation message, if any, a `<label>`
      around the legend and the widget, and a `<span class="required">*</span>`, so a
      stylesheet can style the alert and the marker. The marker is currently shown on every
      row, whether or not the field is required.

entry .Formulation   how a form, and each labelled row of it, is framed in HTML
  detail
      A `Formulation` is the presentation of a form, separate from how its fields are derived:
      `#form` wraps the rows and the submit button, and `#element` frames one widget with its
      legend and validation message. `elicit` and `edit` take one as a contextual value;
      `formulations.postFormulation` is the standard one, and an application defines its own
      for its own markup:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import htmlDoms.whatwg, whatwg.*

      given Formulation:
        def form(content: List[Html of Flow], submit: Optional[Text]): Html of Flow =
          Form(method = t"post")(Html.Fragment(content*), Input.Submit(value = submit.or(t"Go")))

        def element
          ( widget: Html of Phrasing, legend: Text, validation: Optional[Message], required: Boolean )
        :   Html of Flow =
          Div(Label(legend, widget))
      ```

      A `Formulation` is capture-tracked (`Findable`), so one may close over a capability.
  refer legerdemain.formulations
  entry #form      wraps a form's rows and its submit button, labelled with the text or a default
  entry #element   frames one widget with its legend, its validation message, and whether it is required

entry .Formulaic   a type which can be presented as form rows: derived for case classes, field by field
  detail
      `Formulaic` is the typeclass `elicit` and `edit` need. It is derived for a product type
      from its fields — each field must itself be `Formulaic` — and is given for any single
      value which is `Elicitable` and whose widget is `Renderable` as phrasing HTML. A
      derived product renders as a `<fieldset>` with a `<legend>`, so nesting in the data is
      nesting in the form.

      `#fields` receives the `Pointer` of the value within the whole form, its legend, the
      part of the submitted `Query` under that pointer, the `Validation` and the `Formulation`;
      a single field looks up its own message in the validation by its pointer.
  refer legerdemain.Elicitable
  refer legerdemain.elicit
  entry .elicitable   a single field's row: its `Elicitable` widget, rendered and framed with its message
      Makes the widget for the value with the field's dotted name, legend and submitted value,
      renders it, and frames it with the formulation's `element`.
  entry .conjunction   derives the rows of a case class: a fieldset of each field's rows
      The derivation `Formulaic` uses for products. Each field's legend is its name split at
      the capitals and capitalised, and its pointer extends the parent's with the field's
      name.
  entry #fields   the form rows for a value, given its pointer, legend, submitted query, validation and formulation

entry .Elicitable   which widget edits a type, and how a value of it is entered as text
  detail
      `value is Elicitable` says what a form field of type `value` looks like: its `Operand` is
      a `Widget` type, `#widget` makes one with a field's name, legend and current value, and
      `#input` gives the text a value is submitted as. The givens in the companion choose:

      - any `Encodable in Text` value — `Text`, numbers, validated types such as
        `EmailAddress` — is a text `Field` (`decodable`, in `Elicitable2`, at lower priority);
      - a `Boolean` is a `Checkbox`, submitted as `on` when checked (`boolean`);
      - an `Enumerable` type is a `Combobox` offering each value's name (`enumerable`);
      - a `Reference[Entity]` to a stored entity is a `Dropdown` of every stored entity,
        shown by its `Showable` rendering (`reference`).

      A type of one's own gets a different widget with a given of its own.
  refer legerdemain.Widget
  refer legerdemain.Formulaic
  entry .boolean      edits a `Boolean` with a `Checkbox`, entered as `on` when true and empty when false
  entry .enumerable   edits an `Enumerable` type with a `Combobox` of its values' names
      Each value is entered by its name, as `Enumerable` names it, and the combobox's list
      offers all of them.
  entry .reference    edits a `Reference` to a stored entity with a `Dropdown` of all such entities
      Needs the entity to be `Listable`, `Showable` and `Referenceable`, and its reference's
      operand to be `Encodable in Text`: every stored entity is enumerated, each option keyed
      by its encoded reference and labelled with the entity's `show`.
  entry #input    the text a value of this type is entered or submitted as
  entry #widget   the widget for a field, from its name, its legend and its current text

entry .Elicitable2   the lower-priority `Elicitable` given, for any type encodable as text
  detail
      Mixed into the `Elicitable` companion so that the more specific givens there win.
  entry #decodable   edits any `Encodable in Text` value with a text `Field`, entered as its encoding
      Despite its name, it requires only that the type is `Encodable in Text`: the field shows
      the value's encoding, and the submission is decoded separately, through `Query`.

entry .Widget   a form control, with the name it is submitted under and its current value
  detail
      The common shape of `Field`, `Checkbox`, `Combobox`, `Dropdown` and `RadioGroup`: each is
      a case class with a `#name`, the field's dotted path in the form, and a `#value`, the text
      it holds, and each companion has a `Renderable` given which renders it as phrasing HTML.
  entry #name    the name the control is submitted under: the field's dotted path within the form
  entry #value   the text the control holds, from the submitted query or the value being edited

entry .Field   a text input, the widget for any value encodable as text
  detail
      Renders as `<input type="text">` with the widget's name and value; the label is
      rendered by the `Formulation` around it.
  entry .renderable   renders a field as an `<input type="text">`
  entry #label   the field's legend
  entry #name    the name the field is submitted under: its dotted path within the form
  entry #value   the text in the field

entry .Checkbox   a checkbox, the widget for a `Boolean`
  detail
      Renders as `<input type="checkbox">`, checked when its value is not empty. A checked box
      is submitted as `on` and an unchecked one not at all, which is why a `Boolean` decodes
      from a `Query` by whether its key is present.
  entry .renderable   renders a checkbox, checked if its value is non-empty
  entry #name    the name the box is submitted under: its dotted path within the form
  entry #value   `on` when checked, and empty otherwise

entry .Combobox   a text input with a list of suggestions, the widget for an enumeration
  detail
      Renders as an `<input>` tied by its `list` attribute to a `<datalist>` of the options, so
      a browser suggests the values while still accepting typed text, which then must decode.
      The datalist's id is the widget's name, so the name must be a valid DOM id: a field whose
      name contains whitespace, or a lone enumeration at the root of a form (whose name is
      empty), cannot be rendered.
  entry .renderable   renders a combobox as an `<input>` and its `<datalist>`
  entry #name      the name the input is submitted under, and its datalist's id
  entry #options   the suggested values
  entry #value     the text in the input

entry .Dropdown   a selection from a fixed list of labelled options, the widget for a stored reference
  detail
      Renders as a `<select>` of `<option>`s, each submitting its `key` and showing its `value`.
  entry .renderable   renders a dropdown as a `<select>` of its options
  entry #name      the name the selection is submitted under
  entry #options   the options, each a submitted key and a displayed label
  entry #value     the key currently selected

entry .RadioGroup   a set of radio buttons sharing one name, one of which is chosen
  detail
      Renders as a `<label>` per option around an `<input type="radio">`. No standard
      `Elicitable` produces one; a type which should be chosen by radio buttons needs an
      `Elicitable` given whose `Operand` is `RadioGroup`.
  entry .renderable   renders a radio group as a labelled radio button per option
  entry #name      the name every button in the group is submitted under
  entry #options   the options, each with a key, a submitted value and a label
  entry #value     the value currently chosen

entry .Query   form-encoded key–value pairs: a URL query string or a form submission, and their codecs
  detail
      A `Query` is an ordered list of `(key, value)` pairs of `Text`, as a query string
      `a=1&b=2` or an `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` body holds them. Nested data is
      flattened into dotted keys — `leader.name` — so a case class encodes to a `Query` field
      by field and decodes from one, which is how form submissions and query parameters become
      typed values.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label An empty query
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val query = Query()
            query.show   // t""
      frame
        label One parameter
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val query = Query(List(t"name" -> t"Ada Lovelace"))
            query.show   // t"name=Ada+Lovelace"
      frame
        label A second parameter appended
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            val query = Query(List(t"name" -> t"Ada Lovelace")) + Query(List(t"email" -> t"ada@example.com"))
            query.show   // t"name=Ada+Lovelace&email=ada%40example.com"
      frame
        label Both nested under a prefix, then decoded
        code
            import soundness.*
            import strategies.throwUnsafely

            case class Person(name: Text, email: Text)
            case class Team(leader: Person, title: Text)

            val query =
              (Query(List(t"name" -> t"Ada Lovelace")) + Query(List(t"email" -> t"ada@example.com")))
              . prefix(t"leader") + Query(List(t"title" -> t"Analysts"))

            query.show      // t"leader.name=Ada+Lovelace&leader.email=ada%40example.com&title=Analysts"
            query.as[Team]  // Team(Person(t"Ada Lovelace", t"ada@example.com"), t"Analysts")
      ##
      ```

      Text decodes to a `Query` with `as[Query]` and a `Query` encodes, or `show`s, as the
      URL-encoded string. Any type which is `Encodable in Text` encodes to a `Query` of one
      unnamed value, and any case class whose fields encode does so field by field; decoding
      mirrors both, raising `Query.Error` for a missing parameter. Under an accruing strategy
      (`validate`), every missing or invalid field of a case class is reported, each with its
      dotted `Pointer`, and the case class's constructor runs only if every field decoded.

      Named parameters are read and written dynamically — `query.name`, `query.name = value`,
      `Query.make(name = value)` — when a `Parametric` given types the name.
  refer legerdemain.Parametric
  refer legerdemain.elicit
  entry .apply   an empty query, a query of one unnamed value, or one of the given pairs, in order
      `Query()` is empty, `Query(text)` holds the single value `text` with an empty key — the
      shape a value `Encodable in Text` encodes to — and `Query(pairs)` holds a list of `(key,
      value)` pairs as they are.
  entry .encodable   encodes a `Query` as URL-encoded text; and any encodable value or case class as a `Query`
      Two givens share the name. One renders a `Query` as `key=value` pairs joined by `&`, each
      key and value URL-encoded. The other, an inline given, makes any type `Encodable in
      Query`: a type `Encodable in Text` becomes a query of one unnamed value, and a case
      class encodes each field under its name, nested fields under dotted keys.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      summon[Person is Encodable in Query].encoded(Person(t"Jack", 12)).show   // t"name=Jack&age=12"
      ```
  entry .decodable   decodes URL-encoded text as a `Query`; and a `Query` as any decodable value or case class
      Two givens share the name. One splits text on `&` and each part at its first `=`,
      URL-decoding both sides; a part with no `=` is a key with an empty value. The other, an
      inline given, makes any type `Decodable in Query`: a type `Decodable in Text` is decoded
      from the query's unnamed value, and a case class field by field from the parameters
      under its fields' names.

      A missing value raises `Query.Error` with the reason `Missing`, unless the type has a
      `Default`, which is used after the error is raised. Under an accruing strategy, every
      missing field of a case class is raised, each focused at its dotted `Pointer`, and the
      constructor is not run on fallback values — so a refined type's constructor never
      reports phantom errors for fields the user did not fill in.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Person(name: Text, email: Text)

      t"name=Ada&email=a%40b.c".as[Query].as[Person]   // Person(t"Ada", t"a@b.c")
      ```
  entry .EncodableDerivation   the product derivation of `Encodable in Query`: each field encoded under its name
    detail
        Used by the inline `encodable` given for case classes; not normally named directly.
    entry .conjunction   encodes a product as the union of its fields' queries, each prefixed with the field's name
  entry .DecodableDerivation   the product derivation of `Decodable in Query`: each field decoded from its parameters
    detail
        Used by the inline `decodable` given for case classes; not normally named directly.
        It decodes the fields in a single traversal, focuses each field's errors at its
        `Pointer` (a nested record's error lands at `outer.inner`), and constructs the product
        only when every field decoded cleanly.
    entry .conjunction   decodes a product field by field, constructing it only if every field decoded
  entry .booleanEncodable   encodes a `Boolean` as a checkbox does: `on` when true, and nothing when false
  entry .booleanDecodable   decodes a `Boolean` as a checkbox's submission: true if the value is present at all
      An unchecked checkbox is not submitted, so a `Boolean` field is never missing: its
      absence is `false`.
  entry .showable   shows a `Query` as its URL-encoded query string, as `#queryString` gives it
  entry .inspectable   a debug rendering of a `Query`: `key = "value"` pairs, separated by commas, not URL-encoded
  entry .applyDynamicNamed   `Query.make(name = value, …)`: a query from named, typed parameters
      Each parameter name must have a `Parametric` given in scope saying what type its value
      takes, and each value must be `Encodable in Query`; both are checked as the code
      compiles. A value of the wrong type is a compile error, _the parameter `name` takes
      values of `T` but the provided value had type `U`_; a name with no `Parametric` is
      another (error 865), and a value with no encoding a third (error 790).

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      inline given ("key" is Parametric to Text) = !!
      inline given ("count" is Parametric to Int) = !!

      Query.make(key = t"hello world", count = 24).show   // t"key=hello+world&count=24"
      ```

      Telekinesis's `queryParameters.arbitraryQueryParameter`, imported by name, accepts any
      name with any value.
  entry .addable   `query + query`: the pairs of both, the left's first
  entry .Error   raised when a parameter a value needs is missing from a query
    detail
        Error 205: _the query parameter could not be read because the parameter was not
        present in the query string_. Raised by the `Decodable in Query` givens when a value
        which is required has no parameter, and focused at that field's `Pointer` when a case
        class is decoded, so an accruing strategy reports every missing field of a form.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        case class Person(name: Text, email: Text)

        capture[Query.Error](t"name=Ada".as[Query].as[Person]).reason   // Query.Error.Reason.Missing
        ```
    entry .Reason   why a query parameter could not be read: `Missing`, the only reason
      detail
          `Missing` (1): the parameter was not present in the query string.
      entry #number   the reason's number, the second part of the error's code
    entry .communicable   renders a reason as a message: _the parameter was not present in the query string_
    entry #reason   the `Query.Error.Reason`
  entry #values   the query's `(key, value)` pairs, in order, with keys dotted for nested fields
  entry #append   a query with another's pairs after this one's
  entry #nil      whether the query holds no pairs
  entry #++       a query with another's pairs after this one's, as `#append`
  entry #selectDynamic   `query.name`: the parameter `name`, decoded to the type its `Parametric` names
      Needs `"name" is Parametric to Result` and `Result is Decodable in Query`; the part of the
      query under `name` (its own value and any dotted children) is decoded, raising
      `Query.Error` if it is missing.
  entry #updateDynamic   `query.name = value`: a query with the parameter `name` added, encoded from a typed value
      Needs `"name" is Parametric to Result` and the value to be `Encodable in Query`. A value
      encoding to one unnamed value is added under `name`; a structured one is added under
      `name.` and each of its own keys. The receiver is unchanged.
  entry #at      the value under a key, decoded from text, or `Unset` if there is none
      `query.at[Int](t"page")` is the value of the parameter `page` as an `Int`, or `Unset`
      when no `page` parameter has a value of its own. It needs a `Tactic` for the decoding.
  entry #as      the whole query decoded as a value, with errors focused at their `Pointer`s
      `query.as[Person]` decodes through `Person is Decodable in Query`, so a missing field
      raises `Query.Error` focused at that field's dotted pointer.
  entry #apply   the query's unnamed value, or `Unset`; or the part of the query under a key
      `query()` is the value of a query which holds exactly one pair with an empty key, and
      `Unset` otherwise. `query(t"leader")` is the sub-query under `leader`: the pair keyed
      `leader` becomes the unnamed value and each key beginning `leader.` loses that prefix.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val query = t"leader.name=Ada&leader.email=a%40b.c&title=Analysts".as[Query]
      query(t"leader")             // the query name=Ada&email=a%40b.c
      query(t"leader")(t"name")()  // t"Ada"
      ```
  entry #prefix   the query with every key put under a prefix: `name` becomes `prefix.name`
      An unnamed value's key becomes the prefix itself, so a value encoded on its own and then
      prefixed is a named parameter.
  entry #queryString   the query as URL-encoded text, with an unnamed value written alone, without `=`
      Each pair is `key=value`, URL-encoded, joined with `&`; a pair with an empty key is
      written as its value alone.

entry .Parametric   the type a named query parameter takes, for `query.name` and `Query.make(name = …)`
  detail
      `"name" is Parametric to Result` declares that the query parameter `name` holds values of
      type `Result`. It is the evidence the dynamic accessors of `Query` need — `query.name`,
      `query.name = value` and `Query.make(name = value)` — so a parameter is read and written
      at a fixed type, checked as the code compiles. It carries no behaviour, so a given is
      written as an erased placeholder:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      inline given ("page" is Parametric to Int) = !!
      ```

      Telekinesis's `queryParameters.arbitraryQueryParameter` gives every name every type, for
      code which wants dynamic access without declaring each parameter.
  refer legerdemain.Query
  entry #Self   the parameter's name, as a literal `Label` type

entry .internal   the macro behind `Query.make`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macro has a stable home.
  entry .query   the macro behind `Query.make(name = value, …)`: checks each name's `Parametric` and encodes each value
